    story: "After sitting beneath the Bodhi tree, Siddhartha entered deep meditation, vowing that even if his flesh and bones should wither, he would not rise until he solved the root of human suffering. Mara, the lord of illusion and desire, unleashed terrifying armies of demons, storms of burning coals, and seductive apparitions to break his focus. Smiling with profound equanimity, Siddhartha simply touched his hand to the earth (Bhumisparsha), calling the earth to witness his lifetimes of selfless virtue. The illusions instantly vanished, and as the morning star rose, he awoke as the Buddha.",
    interfaithEcho: "The Buddha's absolute victory over the seductive illusions and terrors of Mara under the Bodhi tree directly mirrors Jesus' forty days of resisting the temptations in the wilderness, showing the universal battle over the ego.",
    imageUrl: "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1528127269322-539801943592?auto=format&fit=crop&w=600&q=80",
    imageCaption: "A serene hand-carved golden Buddha statue meditating deeply, representing total mindfulness and the cessation of suffering under the Bodhi Tree."
