    detailedEthos: "Gilgamesh represents the eternal human struggle against mortality, existential despair, and the transformation of raw pride into humble wisdom. Initially self-centered, his deep grief for his companion leads him to explore the physical and metaphysical ends of the earth, eventually discovering that true immortality is found not in avoiding physical death, but in the enduring monuments and peaceful legacies build for one's people.",
    story: "After crossing the waters of death, Gilgamesh sought the sage Utnapishtim, who challenged him to defeat sleep if he wished to defeat death. Gilgamesh sat down, but weary from his travels, instantly fell asleep, sleeping for seven days. Upon waking, he realized his mortality. Utnapishtim then gifted him a youth-renewing flower from the ocean floor. While Gilgamesh bathed, a serpent stole the flower, eating it and shedding its skin. Returning empty-handed to his home of Uruk, Gilgamesh gazed upon its massive walls with clear eyes, finally achieving peace by dedicating himself to his kingdom.",
