    scriptureRef: { bookKey: "homers_iliad", bookTitle: "The Iliad" },
    summary: "The greatest Greek warrior of the Trojan War, son of the sea-nymph Thetis. Renowned for his near-invulnerability, fiery pride, and devastating rage, his fated choice between a short, glorious life and a long, quiet one sits at the heart of classical epic literature.",
    detailedEthos: "Achilles represents the tension between mortal transience and the pursuit of eternal glory (kleos). He is an intensely emotional character, moved by absolute pride and loyalty to those he loves. His near-godlike physical strength is contrasted directly by his emotional vulnerability and his tragic awareness of his own fated early death under the walls of Troy.",
    story: "Blinded by fury at the death of his beloved companion Patroclus, Achilles slew Hector, the Trojan prince, and dragged his body behind his chariot for twelve days in an act of desecration. That night, King Priam of Troy secretly slipped into the Greek camp, kissed Achilles' hands, and begged for his son's corpse. Looking at the old king, Achilles wept, remembering his own father, and recognized their shared suffering and mortality. He lifted Priam up and returned Hector's body, revealing a deep, transcending core of basic human empathy.",
    interfaithEcho: "Achilles' sudden, beautiful breakthrough of grief-driven empathy for his enemy Priam mirrors the epic moments in the Mahabharata where warriors recognize the shared human soul on both sides of a cataclysmic battle, demonstrating that deep sorrow can wipe away political and national enmity.",
